  "metadata": {
    "title": "Merge sort: split, sort each half, then merge",
    "subject": "Computer Science",
    "topic": "Merge Sort",
    "objective": "Show divide, sort halves and merge in order.",
    "learner": "Can read recursion and arrays.",
    "misconceptions": [
      "Merge sort is in-place",
      "Each split sorts its part immediately",
      "The merge sorts the whole array again"
    ],
    "expectedUnderstanding": [
      "The array is split in half before any sorting; splitting alone changes nothing about order.",
      "Each half is sorted independently, and the merge may start only when both halves are sorted.",
      "The merge repeatedly compares the two front elements and appends the smaller one, so it is linear in the two half-lengths.",
      "The extra output row is real storage: merge sort is not in-place, and this scene does not claim constant memory."
    ]
  },
