      "pause": true,
      "learnerAction": "answer"
    },
    {
      "id": "cp-first",
      "at": 8.8,
      "title": "Which front",
      "body": "The merge is about to start. The two fronts are 27 and 3. Which value is written first, and which comparison decides it?",
      "pause": true,
      "learnerAction": "answer"
    },
    {
      "id": "cp-end",
      "at": 15.2,
      "title": "The final order",
      "body": "Say the final sequence out loud. How many values did each merge step compare, and did any value move before its half was sorted?",
      "pause": true,
      "learnerAction": "answer"
    }
  ],
  "narration": [
    {
      "at": 0.8,
      "text": "Six values, unsorted. Merge sort splits the array before it sorts anything; splitting alone does not change the order."
    },
    {
      "at": 3.2,
      "text": "Two halves of three. Each half is now sorted on its own: 27, 38, 43 and 3, 9, 82."
    },
    {
      "at": 6.6,
      "text": "Both halves are sorted, but the array as a whole is not. The merge is the step that puts them together."
    },
    {
      "at": 9.4,
      "text": "The merge looks only at the two front values. 3 is smaller than 27, so 3 is written first, then 9, then 27, and so on."
    },
    {
      "at": 15.4,
      "text": "Each value is compared at most once per merge, so combining two sorted halves is linear. The extra output row is storage: merge sort is not in-place."
    }
  ],
  "fallback": {
    "text": "Static array diagram. Top row: 38, 27, 43, 82, 9, 3 with a divider after the third value, labelled left half and right half. Left half after sorting: 27, 38, 43. Right half after sorting: 3, 9, 82. The merge compares the fronts in this order: 3 against 27, then 9 against 27, then 27 against 82, then 38 against 82, then 43 against 82, then 82. Bottom output row: 3, 9, 27, 38, 43, 82. Arrows show each value moving to the output only after both halves are sorted. Caption: split, sort each half, then merge by repeatedly taking the smaller front; the output row is extra storage, so merge sort is not in-place."
  },
  "accessibility": {
    "alt": "An array of six boxes, 38, 27, 43, 82, 9 and 3, first shown unsorted. A dashed divider then separates the left half 38, 27, 43 from the right half 82, 9, 3. The values in each half move into sorted order, giving 27, 38, 43 on the left and 3, 9, 82 on the right. Then the merge moves values one at a time down to an output row: 3, 9, 27, 38, 43, 82, always taking the smaller of the two current fronts. Checkpoints pause before the split, after both halves are sorted, and before the first merge pick.",
